 "つったらぱったら": "مَشْغُول, مَشْغُول (Crowded with business or activities; having a great deal going on.)  [busy]\nYamagata dialect",
 "つっつく": "خَزَّ (To prod or jab with an object such as a finger or a stick.)  [to poke (repeatedly, lightly)]\nمثال: ハゲタカが突っつく死んだ鹿とか、他の動物の食べ残しとか、 そういう腐った肉を死肉と書きました。\n→ A dead deer being pecked by vultures, remains partly eaten by other animals, that sort of rotten meat is called 'carrion'. / [to peck at (one's food) ; to pick at] / [to peck at (someone's faults, etc.)] / [to egg on ; to put up to]",
 "つっと": "بِسُرْعَة, عَاجِلًا (Rapidly; with speed; fast; with expedition.)  [quickly]\nつうっと is emphatic",
 "つっぱしる": "سُرْعَة (The state of moving quickly or the capacity for rapid motion.) / سُرْعَة (The rate of motion or action, specifically the magnitude of the velocity; the ra)  [to speed]\nمثال: 違反のスピードを出して、突っ走っていた車がブレーキをかけたが、空回りして、運転手があっというまもなく、トラックの背後に、もろにぶつかった。\n→ The speeding vehicle skidded and crashed head-on into the rear-end of a truck before the driver could say Jack Robinson. / [to charge ahead (in pursuit of some goal) ; to push forward]",
